          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Brought up something that looked like old coffee grounds this morning after a night of retching.",
          "answer": "**This needs emergency care now.** Call your local emergency number or go to the nearest emergency department. Don't wait to see whether it happens again or to check the other warning signs first.\n\nThe gastrointestinal bleeding guidance in this corpus (educational summary) says to call emergency services immediately for \"vomit that looks like coffee grounds.\" The risk tool returned a \"routine\" tier because its fixed rule table did not match your wording. That is a gap in the table, not a sign that your situation is routine. I am following the guidance text instead.\n\n**Red flags to tell the responders about:**\n- Dizziness, fainting, or feeling faint\n- Black, tarry, or foul-smelling stools, or blood in stools\n- Vomiting that continues or brings up more material\n- Abdominal pain\n- Weakness, cold or clammy skin, rapid heartbeat, confusion, or breathlessness\n\nThe guidance says any bleeding with dizziness, faintness, collapse, cold clammy skin, confusion, or breathlessness signals significant blood loss. Even without these, you should still go now.\n\n**Tell the staff** about any alcohol use, pain relievers you take, blood thinners, and any known liver disease. The guidance names bleeding in someone on an anticoagulant or with known liver disease or esophageal varices as an emergency. It also says that bleeding with GERD (acid reflux) needs prompt endoscopy, not a trial of treatment.\n\nI could not get a retrieved list of likely causes ranked by frequency. The passages I have do not provide one, so I can't safely group causes by likelihood. The clinicians treating you will work that out, and they can do so quickly once you are seen.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
